  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 117 AM EDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nA cluster of thunderstorms can be noted across portions of Ohio \nas latest satellite imagery paints mostly clear skies across the \nsouthern Appalachians this evening. Mostly clear skies will likely\nresult in some patchy fog across CWA, though, latest HREF \nprobabilities of visibility less than 4 miles is not nearly as \nwidespread as what was observed this past morning. This is likely \ndue to the lack of precip observed across the area today. While \nmostly dry, the NAM3km has been initializing the activity to our \nnorth fairly well this evening and suggest a few morning \nshower/storms cannot be ruled out in SW VA/NE TN, with the very \nlow chance of some strong wind gusts.\n\nOn the synoptic scale, we are generally expected to remain in a \nnorthwest flow pattern through Thursday as we sit beneath that \ntransition zone between a strong ridge axis to our west and a \ntrough axis to our north and east. Unfortunately, this pattern \npromotes lower confidence levels when really trying to hone in on \nexact hazards and timing of expected rounds of showers and \nthunderstorms, as each round(or lack thereof) can impact the next.\n\nLatest Hi-res guidance suggest the potential for convective \ninitiation in the early to mid-afternoon. If these storms are able\nto develop, ample instability will promote potential for damaging\nwind gusts, isolated flooding, and perhaps small hail as the \nprimary hazards. While I suppose the threat of a brief spin-up is \nnot zero with the afternoon activity, shear is more borderline at \nthat timeframe. A more pronounced vort max is expected to swing \ninto the Ohio Valley this evening/tonight, with the potential for \nan MCS to bring increased coverage of showers and storms. Though \ntiming is fairly broad with the latest HREF suite depicting \nreally anytime between about 00-12Z, or some models not even \ninitializing this second round. Damaging wind and flooding will \nremain the primary hazard, but with enhanced shear due to an \nincrease in the low-level jet, a spin-up or two would be possible \nconditional on the activity not moving through too late into the \novernight so that a surface inversion begins to develop. \nAdditional rounds of showers and storms are possible Wednesday and\nThursday, as more bouts of energy translate through mean flow, \nbut timing is highly uncertain. With a warm and moist airmass, \ncannot rule out localized flooding and strong to damaging winds \nwith any activity.\n\nThe upper level ridge will shift eastward Friday into the \nweekend, and while some precipitation chances continue, the chance\nfor any organized MCSs trends downward while temperatures trend \nslightly warmer.",
